WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.719 --> 00:00:07.220
To make it easy to sychronise
the audio and video later.

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:09.260
Except... that's not really an
answer to the question, is it?

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:12.380
If the question was "why do YouTubers
clap at the start of their recordings",

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:14.830
then yes, that's the answer,
it's to help with synchronisation.

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:17.930
A lot of the time, audio and video
are being recorded separately.

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:19.609
The microphone on my collar
is sending its signal

00:00:19.609 --> 00:00:23.070
to a small and very beaten-up recorder
that's clipped to my back pocket.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:25.349
The camera is recording its own sound too,

00:00:25.349 --> 00:00:27.539
and saving that as part of the video file
on its memory card,

00:00:27.539 --> 00:00:29.069
[muffled, through camera mic]
but it sounds terrbile.

00:00:29.069 --> 00:00:31.089
[back to main microphone]
So when I'm working on the edit

00:00:31.089 --> 00:00:32.689
and putting the two files back together,

00:00:32.689 --> 00:00:35.270
it's really useful to have
a short, loud, impulse sound

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:36.770
at the start of both files,

00:00:36.770 --> 00:00:39.480
so I can synchronise them just by
looking for the spike on the display.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:42.220
There's a couple of extra complications
there, though.

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:43.220
First of all:

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:44.970
should that synchronisation be perfect?

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:47.460
That camera is about two metres
away from me.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:49.380
The speed of sound changes
a little with temperature,

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:52.360
but roughly, that's about
6 or 7 milliseconds.

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:53.510
Not enough to care about here,

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:55.480
but once you start putting
the camera further away,

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:57.230
ten or twenty metres for long shots...

00:00:57.230 --> 00:01:01.970
that sound can take a couple of frames
to reach the camera and the audience.

00:01:01.970 --> 00:01:02.970
That would feel weird,

00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:04.380
so I'd have to slip the audio a little.

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:06.740
That's why it's worth double-checking
the synchronisation by looking

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:10.110
for the frame of video
where the hands touch.

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:12.860
Plus, some cameras,
particularly old GoPros,

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:14.970
actually add some sound delay
to the video file,

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:17.070
their synchronisation is terrible.

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:19.720
So if I've got three, or ten,
or a hundred different cameras

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:20.900
all looking at the same thing,

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:23.260
I can use that clap to
synchronise them all:

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:25.100
get it close by looking
for the spike,

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:27.180
confirm it by looking
at the hands.

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:30.120
Actually, worst case, the audio can
actually be late by up to...

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:31.620
maybe three frames or so?

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:34.450
And the chances are no-one
will mind or even notice.

00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:37.110
Actually, the sound on this video
has been three frames late

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:38.680
ever since I cut back
to this shot of me?

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:41.479
If you did actually notice, well done,
I'll put it back to normal now.

00:01:41.479 --> 00:01:44.310
I suspect that works because brains
are used to correcting for

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:47.420
small speed-of-sound lags for
people shouting from a distance,

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:50.740
but that's just speculation,
I can't find research to back that up.

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:52.080
And because the viewer might have things

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:55.690
like a cheap external soundbar
on a television that adds more lag,

00:01:55.690 --> 00:01:57.640
it's best to synchronise
as close as you can.

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:00.780
If the production's got a high budget,
they might use a clapperboard instead.

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:03.049
That means the editor
has a record of which file

00:02:03.049 --> 00:02:05.020
is which take of which scene,
at the start,

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:07.530
and they can sync up the audio
at the same time.

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:09.860
But all that is still not
an answer to the question.

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:11.260
It's the answer everyone gives,

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:13.590
but it's not what the title of
this video is actually asking.

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:16.470
This isn't "why do YouTubers clap
at the start of recordings",

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:19.560
it's "why do YouTubers clap
at the start of videos".

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:20.819
I've done that plenty of times before.

00:02:20.819 --> 00:02:21.950
I did it in this video.

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:24.260
Why do we leave a bit of
technical junk in the video?

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:26.540
Why do I set up a really nice shot
and then ruin it

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:27.540
by showing the setup part,

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:29.090
letting my camera fuzz about with autofocus,

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:31.070
and make the whole video... worse?

00:02:31.070 --> 00:02:34.540
Well, I can't speak for everyone,
but my reason is:

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:36.050
it sets the audience's expectations,

00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:37.980
and it makes it feel more authentic.

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:39.780
When I'm filming on my own
with a static camera,

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:42.950
it can sometimes look like I'm
just stood against a green-screen.

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:46.050
But clapping, walking into frame,
letting the focus settle,

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:47.650
showing that behind-the-scenes moment...

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:50.280
it proves I'm here,
it makes me seem more trustworthy.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:53.319
For someone vlogging from their bedroom,
keeping those claps and the preparation in,

00:02:53.319 --> 00:02:56.760
letting the audience see a little bit
of the slip-ups and how it's made...

00:02:56.760 --> 00:03:00.910
it helps make a connection,
helps make them relatable.

00:03:00.910 --> 00:03:01.910
I don't need to be relatable.

00:03:01.910 --> 00:03:03.540
I don't really want to be relatable?

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:04.569
But trustworthy.

00:03:04.569 --> 00:03:06.990
Trustworthy is good,
and leaving the clap and walk in the video

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:09.550
establishes that I'm
one person with one camera,

00:03:09.550 --> 00:03:11.069
that I don't have a crew on this shoot,

00:03:11.069 --> 00:03:14.980
and that I am actually here,
wherever here is.

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:19.070
Sometimes I'll leave that in
when I actually do have a crew.

00:03:19.070 --> 00:03:20.590
It's all about the aesthetics.

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:22.620
Showing that setup,
leaving that synchronisation clap in,

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:24.470
it's all about fitting
the message to the medium,

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:25.980
planning not just what you want to say,

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:27.610
but the subtext of how you say it,

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:30.430
and handling all the subtle cues
that connect performer to audience

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:32.819
and say, it's okay.

00:03:32.819 --> 00:03:33.400
You can trust me.

